Shadow home secretary Ann Widdecombe received a rapturous reception from party activists today after ripping up her prepared conference speech and savaging the government's policy towards asylum-seekers, its management of prisons and the "falling" number of police.
In the same law and order debate, former chairman Norman Tebbit attacked Labour's Northern Ireland policy. Conservative leader William Hague, meanwhile, attempted to establish a single party line on the euro.
And former prime minister Margaret Thatcher, who arrived in Blackpool in the afternoon to be greeted by Mr Hague and his wife Ffion, went out of her way to quash suggestions she had ever dubbed him "Wee Willie". Asked if she had ever used that phrase, Lady Thatcher replied: "No, it's not the language I ever use."
In a tub-thumping performance at the Tory conference in Blackpool, Miss Widdecombe abandoned the podium and prowled the platform delivering her message without notes.
She told representatives the Tories would extend the prison workshops system and introduce "proper, outside contracts" to make them self-financing. Miss Widdecombe pledged judges and magistrates would be allowed to consider whether or not to take time spent on remand by criminals into account when sentencing.
She said that, under a Labour government, Britain had seen crime rise for the first time in five years. "We built more prisons to deal with the problem - (home secretary) Jack Straw let them out early and tagged them."
Mr Straw's announcement last week that he would recruit 5,000 more officers was a "smoke and mirror deception", Miss Widdecombe declared, because there would be a net decrease of 2,000 over the next three years. She said a Conservative government would reverse the decline, alongside a drive to improve policing coverage in rural areas.
Miss Widdecombe added the government's immigration policy had let down Britain and genuine asylum seekers. "The message that they send out loud and clear to the rest of the world is that Britain is once again a soft touch." In power, the Tories would restore the list of safe countries from which asylum seekers could be "fast tracked".
She claimed many countries were unlikely to produce genuine asylum seekers and applications from their nationals should be examined quickly, with non-genuine applicants being returned home swiftly. There would be no mass amnesties for asylum seekers because "every time there is an amnesty, people come here in the hope of benefiting from the next one".
Turning to prison policy, Miss Widdecombe told representatives rehabilitation was "not some sort of soft, liberal, wet option, but a crucial measure of public protection. There have to be proper, sensible workshops in every single prison... We have to make sure that prison workshops have proper, outside contracts, which will then become self-financing."
She described Labour's attempts to tackle youth crime as "spectacular flops" and pledged to expand tenfold the number of secure training centres. Young offenders would be given flexible sentences with levels of achievement which could earn them early release.
Current sentencing policy was another area of concern for crime victims, Miss Widdecombe said. A Tory government would restore the "honesty in sentencing proposals", which had been "abolished" by Mr Straw. "In future the sentence which is handed down will be the sentence which is served," she said.
William Hague, meanwhile, tried to insist there was no Euro split of any significance in his party. But Conservative ex-chancellor Kenneth Clarke attacked Eurosceptic Tories, accusing senior party figures of breaking with official policy and seeking to rule out joining the euro forever.
He warned the Tories' new policy of renegotiating European treaties where Britain opposed European Union legislation was for some a "cloak" for their real aim of withdrawal from the Union. Former deputy prime minister Michael Heseltine also warned of the "incalculable folly" of letting Eurosceptics push Tories towards a Britain Out policy.
However, Mr Hague insisted his party wanted to stay in Europe and wanted "positive Europeans" in its ranks. He said: "We are not going to eliminate positive Europeans. That is not part of my agenda. My agenda is in Europe, not being run by Europe. People are sick and tired of their powers and rights in this country being handed over to Brussels and I say we have to draw a line and we have to stop that. That is the policy of our party and there is no remaining division of opinion of any significance in our party about this subject."
